{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's claim that separation is necessary is undermined by its own countercondition: if a single engine can verify via symbolic execution that bypasses probabilistic priors, then a same-engine author+checker pair can discover errors without a second training distribution Q. SRC_001 never resolves how 'necessity' survives this admitted self-correction path, leaving the necessity claim overbroad against the problem's requirement to argue whether separation is truly required.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 argues separation is unnecessary under information-theoretic blindness, but restricting the checker to the final claim only does not address the problem's core concern: the same engine's shared training priors. A checker reconstructing a proof from first principles still samples from the same P(Output|Prompt) with the same blind spots as the author. Its own countercondition—shared priors so dominant that any two paths are identical—directly admits this failure mode it never refutes, so the independence it claims is not genuine.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 conflates activation-trajectory divergence with independence, but its own counterconditions expose the flaw: if both paths remain in the same attractor basin or the perturbation cannot overcome self-consistency bias, the perturbation yields only apparent independence. The target offers no evidence within a single run's record that distinguishes genuine independent verification via perturbation from mere restatement under noise—exactly what the problem asks it to identify.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_003"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004 declares separation unnecessary because execution is the only valid verification, but its own countercondition concedes a circular dependency if the same engine authors the test code. This admits that engine identity can poison verification, contradicting the blanket claim that separation is a red herring. The problem explicitly asks whether separating authoring from checking is necessary; SRC_004's own exception shows separation (at least of test authorship) is necessary in the common computable case, so its core claim is self-undermined.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_004"}]}